=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The inter-router physical interface `eth0` on router1 (193.10.11.1/24), which is the sole BGP peering link between AS 1 (router1) and AS 2 (router2).

**Mechanism:** Router1's `eth0` is in `DOWN` state (no `LOWER_UP` flag visible in `ip addr show eth0`). With the physical link down, the Linux kernel immediately tears down any TCP sockets using routes through that interface. bgpd on router1 lost its TCP session to router2 (193.10.11.2:179) and transitioned to `Active` state, with the internal reset reason recorded as *"No path to specified Neighbor"* and FD = -1 (no live socket). Without an established BGP session, router1 holds no route to 200.1.1.0/24, so every packet from pc1 toward pc2 is rejected by router1 with `Destination Net Unreachable`. On the reverse path, router2's session is still nominally `Established` because it is still within the 180-second BGP hold timer (last keepalive received ~2.5 min ago), but the next-hop 193.10.11.1 is unreachable through the downed link, causing router2 to return `Destination Host Unreachable` to pc2.

**Localization:** router1, interface `eth0`. router2 and its bgpd are functioning correctly; the "Established" state on router2 is a transient artefact of the hold timer not yet expiring.

**Root cause:** router1's `eth0` interface was brought down (administratively or by a link-fault injection), severing the BGP peering link and collapsing cross-AS routing.

**Fix:** Bring router1's `eth0` back up — `ip link set eth0 up` on router1. The BGP session will re-establish automatically within seconds, both routers will re-exchange prefixes, and pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity will be restored. There is no need to restart router2.
=== END ===